{
  "summary": "The delta_score metric proposed in round 3 sounds rigorous but introduces its own problems. First, computing semantic similarity between cycle summaries is itself an LLM judgment call — asking an LLM to score 'how many new points were raised' is no more objective than asking it to judge convergence directly; it just adds a numeric veneer to the same subjective assessment, while costing an additional API call per cycle. The thresholds (0.2 for stall, 0.7 for progress) are arbitrary magic numbers that will need per-project tuning, and miscalibration means the system either terminates too early (losing value) or too late (wasting budget) — the very problem the metric was supposed to solve. Second, the proposal to avoid feeding synthesis back into subsequent cycles creates an information loss problem: agents in cycle N+1 only see raw summaries from cycle N, not the synthesized resolution of contradictions, so they may re-litigate already-resolved disagreements, producing circular discussion that the delta_score would paradoxically classify as 'progress' (since re-raised points look like new points in a different framing). Third, the budget_tokens cap is a blunt instrument that doesn't account for the actual value of continued ideation — a complex architectural decision may legitimately need more cycles than a UI tweak, but a flat token budget treats them identically. A simpler and more robust approach would be: keep the current fixed-round structure but make the round count configurable per-item (defaulting to the global config), and let the synthesis agent append a 'confidence' field and 'open_questions' list. If confidence is below a threshold or open questions remain, the item gets flagged for human review rather than automated re-cycling — this respects that genuine architectural disagreements often need human judgment, not more LLM rounds."
